  {
    "label": "figure_title",
    "text": "Figure 4. Spatial permutation entropy of phase-locked states. We study the spatial permutation entropy of phase-locked states, or q-states, which are solutions given by: θ(q) = 2πqj. In this case, q = 0 representing the phase synchronized state, and |q| > 0 representing waves in the network. For patterns with low-spatial frequency (low |q|), the entropy is low, but as the spatial frequency increases, the entropy also increases. However, as the spatial frequency becomes very high (|q > 34), the entropy decreases again, highlighting the non-monotonic nature of this process. We note these states can be obtained in Kuramoto networks with distance-dependent delays. For the analyses here, we consider the threshold σ = π/30.",
