  {
    "label": "figure_title",
    "text": "Figure 6. Phase-based spatial ordinal analysis distinguishes resting-state brain activity. (a) Resting-state EEG signals from 64 scalp channels are filtered in the α-band (8-12 Hz) and the instantaneous phase is extracted. (b) Spatial permu- tation entropy H(t) computed from the phase dynamics separates eyes-open from eyes-closed conditions when averaged across all volunteers. (c-d) The framework applied to two representative individual participants. Top: H(t) reliably distinguishes the two conditions in both cases, here represented by the density of the normalized entropy. Bottom: Pattern probability distri- butions show that eyes-open recordings yield broadly distributed patterns and higher entropy, whereas eyes-closed recordings are dominated by pattern 7, reflecting increased spatial synchronization and lower entropy. (e-f) Across all 109 volunteers. the eyes-open state exhibits systematically higher entropy than eyes-closed in 104 cases. For the analyses here, we consider the threshold σ = π/18.",
